  "an8.30:3.11": "“Objectification” is a translation of <i>papañca.</i> Although in some circles <i>papañca</i> has come to mean a proliferation of thinking, in the Canon it refers not to the amount of thinking, but to a type of thinking marked by the classifications and perceptions it uses. As <a href='https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14'>Sn 4:14</a> points out, the root of these classifications and perceptions is the thought, “I am the thinker.” From this assumption grow such classifications as “me/not me,” “existing/not existing,” which frame experience in terms conducive to further becoming. <a href='https://suttacentral.net/dn21'>DN 21</a> and <a href='https://suttacentral.net/mn18'>MN 18</a> discuss the relationship between objectification and conflict. <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an4.173'>AN 4:173</a> states that the range of objectification is identical with the range of the six sense media. <a href='https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1'>SN 43</a> lists non-objectification as one of many epithets for unbinding.",
